<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>How many grams of protein do I need per day?</h3>
    <p>A general starting point is 1.6 to 2.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight for active people doing regular resistance training. At the lower end suits people in maintenance; the higher end suits those actively trying to build muscle or cut fat while preserving mass. The macro calculator uses your calorie targets to derive a gram number aligned with your goal.</p>

    <h3>Do I need to hit my macros exactly every day?</h3>
    <p>Hitting them closely matters more than hitting them exactly. Being within 5 to 10 grams of each target consistently over a week produces the same results as perfection would. Focus on the weekly average rather than treating every single day as pass or fail.</p>

    <h3>What is the best macro split for weight loss?</h3>
    <p>Higher protein and moderate fat with lower carbs is what research generally supports for fat loss while preserving muscle. The cutting split in this calculator applies roughly a 40/30/30 protein/carbs/fat ratio. That said, the total calorie deficit matters far more than the exact split. You will lose fat at any macro ratio as long as you are eating fewer calories than you burn.</p>

    <h3>Should I use net carbs or total carbs?</h3>
    <p>Total carbs are what the macro calculator uses. If you follow a keto diet you would track net carbs, which subtract fiber from the total since fiber is not absorbed the same way. For most non-keto approaches total carbohydrates is the relevant number to track.</p>
</section>

